  "turns": [
    {
      "turn": 1,
      "doctor_question": "Hello, I'm Dr. Smith. It's nice to meet you; could you tell me what brings you in to see me today?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S022",
      "patient_response": "Well, Dr. Smith, it's nice to meet you too, though to be perfectly honest, I'm not entirely sure I need to be here, but my supervisor insisted. See, I work as a personal care aide, taking care of elderly folks mostly, which is a really big responsibility, you know? You really have to be on your toes because they can slip and fall, and if they fall, it's a whole disaster—hip fractures, hospital visits, paperwork, the works. \n\nSo anyway, we were having this staff meeting last Tuesday, or maybe it was Wednesday... no, Tuesday, because it was right after the lunch shift. We were sitting around talking about safety protocols, and I guess... I guess what brings me in is I just got really, really worked up about it. I mean, it's life or death for some of these clients! So I stood up, and I started acting out this whole fall-prevention drill right there in the middle of the room. I grabbed a couple of the folding chairs to show how to block a sliding fall, and I was waving my arms around to demonstrate how you have to shift your center of gravity, just really getting into the mechanics of it all to make sure everyone understood. \n\nAnd the thing is, I just kept going. People were looking at me kind of funny, and my manager told me to sit down, like, three or four times, but I couldn't seem to stop. I just had to finish showing them exactly how it needed to be done, even with folks telling me to sit down and lower my voice. I was just in the zone with it, but they thought it was too much, so... now here I am.",
