    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Outside of that meeting, have you felt like you've been in an unusually good mood or had much more energy than usual for several days in a row lately?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S033",
      "patient_response": "Well, yeah, I guess you could say that. It’s not just the meeting, honestly. There are these stretches, you know? Like, days at a time where I just wake up and I feel... I don't know, just really bright. Just completely fired up and ready to take on the whole world. It’s like everything is finally lining up perfectly, like all the gears in my head are finally catching and turning exactly how they're supposed to, and I don't even need my coffee to get going. I'll get so much done, whether it's at my apartment or at work. I'll be doing extra rounds, checking on Mrs. Higgins, making sure Mr. Abernathy has his water pitcher filled to the exact brim because hydration is absolutely key for the elderly, right? I just have all this fuel in the tank, and it feels amazing to just go, go, go.\n\nBut the thing is... and this is where it gets a little tricky, I guess, and maybe why my supervisor got so on my case. Because I'm moving so fast and feeling so on top of things, if someone tries to slow me down, or if they start questioning what I'm doing—like asking why I'm reorganizing the supply closet or why I'm explaining the fall protocols so passionately—I just... I snap. I snap way harder than I mean to. It’s like they’re throwing a wrench right into those gears I was talking about. I really don't mean to get nasty with anybody, but when you see everything so clearly and someone else is just dragging their feet or telling you to sit down and be quiet, it just completely sets me off. So yeah, I feel fantastic, like a million bucks, right up until someone gets in my way or doubts me, and then it's just... I just lose my temper with them.",
