      "doctor_question": "Outside of these high-energy periods you've described, have you ever experienced significant stretches of time where you felt deeply down, hopeless, or lost interest in the things you usually enjoy?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Well, no, not really. I mean, I don't get those deep, dark slumps you hear people talk about on those daytime talk shows, you know? Where they just pull the covers up over their heads and can't even drag themselves to the kitchen to make a piece of toast. I don't have those stretches where everything just feels gray and heavy and useless. \n\nI mean, sure, sometimes I might feel a little tired after one of those really intense, high-energy runs where I've been working double shifts and reorganizing the entire supply closet and writing those memos in my head all night. Naturally, your body has to catch up eventually, right? You can't run an engine at a hundred miles an hour forever without it needing a little cool-down period. So maybe I'll have a day where I just want to sit on the couch and watch some TV and not talk to anybody, just kind of recharge the batteries, so to speak. \n\nBut it's never that deep, hopeless feeling you're asking about. I never lose interest in my work or my clients. Even when I'm just having a regular, middle-of-the-road day, I still care about Mrs. Higgins and Mr. Abernathy. I still want to make sure they're safe and taken care of. I don't get that whole 'what's the point of it all' mindset. I always know what the point is. It's just that sometimes I'm tackling the point with a million volts of electricity, and other times I'm just doing it at a normal, steady pace. But yeah, no big, long stretches of feeling completely down in the dumps or hopeless. That's just not really my thing.",
