    {
      "turn": 5,
      "doctor_question": "When you are in those 'middle-of-the-road' periods where you aren't feeling that intense energy or staying up all night, do those voices you hear or those feelings that your supervisor is setting traps for you still happen?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_3",
      "patient_response": "Well, yeah... it’s funny you ask that, because, actually, yes. There was this one stretch not too long ago where things had really leveled out for me. I was having a really calm period, you know? I was actually sleeping normally for once—getting a solid seven or eight hours, going to bed at a decent time instead of being up till dawn. And I wasn't rushing around with all those massive projects either, no overhauling the safety protocols or reorganizing the closets or anything like that. Just going in, doing my job, and coming home. \n\nBut the thing is... for several weeks during that totally normal, calm stretch, that other stuff was still happening. I'd be walking down the hall, totally rested, no racing thoughts at all, and I'd still hear my name being called out. Just out of nowhere, clear as day, when nobody was around. \n\nAnd the stuff with my supervisor... it actually felt like it kind of spread out, because I stayed absolutely convinced that people at work were keeping notes on me. I’d see one of the other aides jotting something down on a clipboard near the nurses' station, and even though my brain wasn't buzzing with all that extra energy, I just knew in my gut they were documenting my every move, writing down what time I took my break or how long I spent in a room, building some kind of file on me. \n\nIt’s strange, right? Because you’d think if the engine cools down, all the extra noise would stop with it. But no, for weeks I was sleeping fine and taking it easy, but I was still hearing my name echoing in the halls and constantly looking over my shoulder, worrying about these secret notes everyone was keeping on me. It really makes you wonder how it all connects, because I was feeling completely even-keeled otherwise, but I still couldn't shake that feeling of being watched and documented.",
